**Space:** O(h) recursion stack
:::

**Pitfall:** Only following right children. Example 2 shows the left subtree can be deeper and visible below the right one.

**Key idea:** Carry the maximum value seen on the path so far down the tree; a node is good when its value is at least that maximum.

**Brute force:** For each node, walk back up its root path (via a parent map) and check that no ancestor is larger. Time O(n·h), Space O(n).

:::details 1. DFS carrying the path maximum
`dfs(node, best)` counts the node if `node.val >= best`, then recurses with `max(best, node.val)`. Each call returns the number of good nodes in its subtree.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int goodNodes(TreeNode root) {
        return dfs(root, root.val);
    }

    private int dfs(TreeNode node, int best) {
        if (node == null) return 0;
        int good = node.val >= best ? 1 : 0;
        best = Math.max(best, node.val);
        return good + dfs(node.left, best) + dfs(node.right, best);
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def goodNodes(self, root: TreeNode) -> int:
        def dfs(node, best):
            if not node:
                return 0
            good = 1 if node.val >= best else 0
            best = max(best, node.val)
            return good + dfs(node.left, best) + dfs(node.right, best)

        return dfs(root, root.val)
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(h) recursion stack
:::

:::details 2. BFS with (node, path max) pairs
The same rule with a queue: each entry remembers the largest value on the path to it. Avoids recursion depth issues on a 10⁵-node chain.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int goodNodes(TreeNode root) {
        int count = 0;
        Deque<TreeNode> nodes = new ArrayDeque<>();
        Deque<Integer> maxes = new ArrayDeque<>();
        nodes.add(root);
        maxes.add(root.val);
        while (!nodes.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode node = nodes.poll();
            int best = maxes.poll();
            if (node.val >= best) count++;
            best = Math.max(best, node.val);
            if (node.left != null) { nodes.add(node.left); maxes.add(best); }
            if (node.right != null) { nodes.add(node.right); maxes.add(best); }
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def goodNodes(self, root: TreeNode) -> int:
        count = 0
        q = deque([(root, root.val)])
        while q:
            node, best = q.popleft()
            if node.val >= best:
                count += 1
            best = max(best, node.val)
            if node.left:
                q.append((node.left, best))
            if node.right:
                q.append((node.right, best))
        return count
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(w), the widest level
:::

**Pitfall:** Using `>` instead of `>=`. A node equal to the path maximum is still good.

**Key idea:** Every node must fit inside an open interval (lo, hi) inherited from its ancestors; equivalently, an in-order traversal of a valid BST is strictly increasing.

**Brute force:** At each node, scan its whole left subtree for anything ≥ it and its whole right subtree for anything ≤ it, then recurse (approach 4). Time O(n²) worst case, Space O(h).

:::details 1. DFS with (lo, hi) bounds
Start with (−∞, +∞). Going left, the node's value becomes the new upper bound; going right, it becomes the new lower bound. A node outside its interval breaks the tree. Use `long` bounds in Java because node values can be `Integer.MIN_VALUE` / `MAX_VALUE`.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isValidBST(TreeNode root) {
        return valid(root, Long.MIN_VALUE, Long.MAX_VALUE);
    }

    private boolean valid(TreeNode node, long lo, long hi) {
        if (node == null) return true;
        if (node.val <= lo || node.val >= hi) return false;
        return valid(node.left, lo, node.val) && valid(node.right, node.val, hi);
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def isValidBST(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> bool:
        def valid(node, lo, hi):
            if not node:
                return True
            if not (lo < node.val < hi):
                return False
            return valid(node.left, lo, node.val) and valid(node.right, node.val, hi)

        return valid(root, float("-inf"), float("inf"))
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(h) recursion stack
:::

:::details 2. Iterative in-order traversal
In-order visits a BST's values in sorted order. Walk it with a stack and remember the previous value; if a value is not strictly larger than the previous one, the tree is invalid.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isValidBST(TreeNode root) {
        Deque<TreeNode> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        Integer prev = null;
        TreeNode node = root;
        while (!stack.isEmpty() || node != null) {
            while (node != null) {
                stack.push(node);
                node = node.left;
            }
            node = stack.pop();
            if (prev != null && node.val <= prev) return false;
            prev = node.val;
            node = node.right;
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def isValidBST(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> bool:
        stack, prev, node = [], None, root
        while stack or node:
            while node:
                stack.append(node)
                node = node.left
            node = stack.pop()
            if prev is not None and node.val <= prev:
                return False
            prev = node.val
            node = node.right
        return True
```

**Time:** O(n), exits early on the first violation
**Space:** O(h) for the stack
:::

:::details 3. BFS with bounds
The bounds rule doesn't depend on visit order, so the (node, lo, hi) triples can go through a queue instead of the call stack.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isValidBST(TreeNode root) {
        Deque<Object[]> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
        q.add(new Object[]{root, Long.MIN_VALUE, Long.MAX_VALUE});
        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            Object[] e = q.poll();
            TreeNode node = (TreeNode) e[0];
            long lo = (long) e[1], hi = (long) e[2];
            if (node == null) continue;
            if (node.val <= lo || node.val >= hi) return false;
            q.add(new Object[]{node.left, lo, (long) node.val});
            q.add(new Object[]{node.right, (long) node.val, hi});
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def isValidBST(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> bool:
        q = deque([(root, float("-inf"), float("inf"))])
        while q:
            node, lo, hi = q.popleft()
            if not node:
                continue
            if not (lo < node.val < hi):
                return False
            q.append((node.left, lo, node.val))
            q.append((node.right, node.val, hi))
        return True
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(w), the widest level
:::

:::details 4. Brute force: check whole subtrees at every node
For each node, verify every value in the left subtree is smaller and every value in the right subtree is larger, then recurse. Simple, but each node is re-scanned by all its ancestors.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isValidBST(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) return true;
        if (!all(root.left, root.val, true) || !all(root.right, root.val, false)) return false;
        return isValidBST(root.left) && isValidBST(root.right);
    }

    // every value in the subtree is < limit (less=true) or > limit (less=false)
    private boolean all(TreeNode node, int limit, boolean less) {
        if (node == null) return true;
        if (less ? node.val >= limit : node.val <= limit) return false;
        return all(node.left, limit, less) && all(node.right, limit, less);
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def isValidBST(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> bool:
        def values(node):
            return values(node.left) + [node.val] + values(node.right) if node else []

        if not root:
            return True
        if any(v >= root.val for v in values(root.left)):
            return False
        if any(v <= root.val for v in values(root.right)):
            return False
        return self.isValidBST(root.left) and self.isValidBST(root.right)
```

**Time:** O(n²) worst case
**Space:** O(h)
:::

**Pitfall:** Only comparing a node with its direct children. Example 3 passes that check but is not a BST.

**Key idea:** In-order traversal of a BST yields values in ascending order, so the k-th node visited in-order is the answer; stop as soon as you reach it.

**Brute force:** Collect every value with any traversal, sort, and index `k − 1` (approach 4). Time O(n log n), Space O(n).

:::details 1. Iterative in-order with a stack
Push the left spine, pop the smallest remaining node, count it, then move to its right child and repeat. Only about h + k nodes are touched.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int kthSmallest(TreeNode root, int k) {
        Deque<TreeNode> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        TreeNode node = root;
        while (!stack.isEmpty() || node != null) {
            while (node != null) {
                stack.push(node);
                node = node.left;
            }
            node = stack.pop();
            if (--k == 0) return node.val;
            node = node.right;
        }
        return -1;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def kthSmallest(self, root: Optional[TreeNode], k: int) -> int:
        stack, node = [], root
        while stack or node:
            while node:
                stack.append(node)
                node = node.left
            node = stack.pop()
            k -= 1
            if k == 0:
                return node.val
            node = node.right
        return -1
```

**Time:** O(h + k)
**Space:** O(h) for the stack
:::

:::details 2. Recursive in-order with a counter
Same order with recursion: left subtree, then this node (decrement k, record the answer when it hits 0), then right subtree. The `k == 0` check stops further work once the answer is found.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private int k, ans = -1;

    public int kthSmallest(TreeNode root, int k) {
        this.k = k;
        inorder(root);
        return ans;
    }

    private void inorder(TreeNode node) {
        if (node == null || k == 0) return;
        inorder(node.left);
        if (--k == 0) {
            ans = node.val;
            return;
        }
        inorder(node.right);
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def kthSmallest(self, root: Optional[TreeNode], k: int) -> int:
        self.k, self.ans = k, -1

        def inorder(node):
            if not node or self.k == 0:
                return
            inorder(node.left)
            self.k -= 1
            if self.k == 0:
                self.ans = node.val
                return
            inorder(node.right)

        inorder(root)
        return self.ans
```

**Time:** O(h + k)
**Space:** O(h) recursion stack
:::

:::details 3. Morris in-order traversal (O(1) space)
Instead of a stack, temporarily point the right pointer of each node's in-order predecessor back at the node ("threading"). The second time you arrive at a node, its left side is done: remove the thread, count the node and go right. This version runs to the end so the tree is fully restored.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int kthSmallest(TreeNode root, int k) {
        TreeNode node = root;
        int ans = -1;
        while (node != null) {
            if (node.left == null) {
                if (--k == 0) ans = node.val; // keep going so every thread is removed
                node = node.right;
            } else {
                TreeNode pred = node.left;
                while (pred.right != null && pred.right != node) pred = pred.right;
                if (pred.right == null) {
                    pred.right = node; // thread back to node
                    node = node.left;
                } else {
                    pred.right = null; // left side done: unthread
                    if (--k == 0) ans = node.val;
                    node = node.right;
                }
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def kthSmallest(self, root: Optional[TreeNode], k: int) -> int:
        node, ans = root, -1
        while node:
            if not node.left:
                k -= 1
                if k == 0:
                    ans = node.val  # keep going so every thread is removed
                node = node.right
            else:
                pred = node.left
                while pred.right and pred.right is not node:
                    pred = pred.right
                if not pred.right:
                    pred.right = node  # thread back to node
                    node = node.left
                else:
                    pred.right = None  # left side done: unthread
                    k -= 1
                    if k == 0:
                        ans = node.val
                    node = node.right
        return ans
```

**Time:** O(n), each edge walked at most a few times
**Space:** O(1)
:::

:::details 4. Brute force: collect and sort
Ignore the BST property: gather all values, sort them and pick index `k − 1`.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int kthSmallest(TreeNode root, int k) {
        List<Integer> vals = new ArrayList<>();
        collect(root, vals);
        Collections.sort(vals);
        return vals.get(k - 1);
    }

    private void collect(TreeNode node, List<Integer> vals) {
        if (node == null) return;
        vals.add(node.val);
        collect(node.left, vals);
        collect(node.right, vals);
    }
}
```

**Python**

