**Time:** O(n log n)
**Space:** O(n)
:::

**Pitfall:** Off-by-one on `k`: it is 1-indexed. Follow-up: if the tree changes often, store subtree sizes in each node so each query is O(h).

**Key idea:** The next preorder value is the root of the current subtree; its position in inorder splits the remaining values into the left and right subtrees. Recurse on the two halves.

**Brute force:** Recurse with array slices and a linear `index()` search for the root (approach 3). Time O(n²), Space O(n²) for the copied slices.

:::details 1. Recursion + hash map of inorder positions
Store each value's inorder index in a hash map. Keep a global pointer into preorder; `build(lo, hi)` takes the next preorder value as root, looks up its inorder index `mid`, then builds the left side from `lo..mid−1` and the right side from `mid+1..hi`. Left must be built first because preorder lists the whole left subtree before the right.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private int pre = 0;
    private int[] preorder;
    private final Map<Integer, Integer> index = new HashMap<>();

    public TreeNode buildTree(int[] preorder, int[] inorder) {
        this.preorder = preorder;
        for (int i = 0; i < inorder.length; i++) index.put(inorder[i], i);
        return build(0, inorder.length - 1);
    }

    // subtree whose in-order slice is inorder[lo..hi]
    private TreeNode build(int lo, int hi) {
        if (lo > hi) return null;
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(preorder[pre++]);
        int mid = index.get(root.val);
        root.left = build(lo, mid - 1);
        root.right = build(mid + 1, hi);
        return root;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def buildTree(self, preorder: List[int], inorder: List[int]) -> Optional[TreeNode]:
        index = {v: i for i, v in enumerate(inorder)}
        self.pre = 0

        def build(lo, hi):  # subtree whose in-order slice is inorder[lo..hi]
            if lo > hi:
                return None
            root = TreeNode(preorder[self.pre])
            self.pre += 1
            mid = index[root.val]
            root.left = build(lo, mid - 1)
            root.right = build(mid + 1, hi)
            return root

        return build(0, len(inorder) - 1)
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(n) for the map, O(h) recursion
:::

:::details 2. Recursion with a stop value (no map)
Walk both arrays with one pointer each. `build(stop)` creates nodes from preorder until the inorder pointer reaches `stop`, the value of the nearest ancestor this subtree hangs to the left of. The left child uses the new node as its stop; the right child inherits the parent's stop.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private int p = 0, i = 0;

    public TreeNode buildTree(int[] preorder, int[] inorder) {
        return build(preorder, inorder, Integer.MIN_VALUE, false);
    }

    // build until we reach `stop` in the in-order sequence (hasStop = false means no limit)
    private TreeNode build(int[] preorder, int[] inorder, int stop, boolean hasStop) {
        if (i >= inorder.length || (hasStop && inorder[i] == stop)) return null;
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(preorder[p++]);
        root.left = build(preorder, inorder, root.val, true);
        i++; // skip root itself in in-order
        root.right = build(preorder, inorder, stop, hasStop);
        return root;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def buildTree(self, preorder: List[int], inorder: List[int]) -> Optional[TreeNode]:
        self.p = self.i = 0

        def build(stop):
            # build until we reach `stop` in the in-order sequence
            if self.i < len(inorder) and inorder[self.i] != stop:
                root = TreeNode(preorder[self.p])
                self.p += 1
                root.left = build(root.val)
                self.i += 1  # skip root itself in in-order
                root.right = build(stop)
                return root
            return None

        return build(None)
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(h) recursion, no extra map
:::

:::details 3. Brute force: slices + linear search
Take `preorder[0]` as root, find it in `inorder` by scanning, slice both arrays into left and right parts and recurse. Clear, but slicing and searching make it quadratic.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public TreeNode buildTree(int[] preorder, int[] inorder) {
        if (preorder.length == 0) return null;
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(preorder[0]);
        int mid = 0;
        while (inorder[mid] != preorder[0]) mid++;
        root.left = buildTree(Arrays.copyOfRange(preorder, 1, mid + 1), Arrays.copyOfRange(inorder, 0, mid));
        root.right = buildTree(Arrays.copyOfRange(preorder, mid + 1, preorder.length),
                               Arrays.copyOfRange(inorder, mid + 1, inorder.length));
        return root;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def buildTree(self, preorder: List[int], inorder: List[int]) -> Optional[TreeNode]:
        if not preorder:
            return None
        root = TreeNode(preorder[0])
        mid = inorder.index(preorder[0])
        root.left = self.buildTree(preorder[1:mid + 1], inorder[:mid])
        root.right = self.buildTree(preorder[mid + 1:], inorder[mid + 1:])
        return root
```

**Time:** O(n²)
**Space:** O(n²) worst case for slices
:::

**Pitfall:** Building the right subtree before the left while using a shared preorder pointer; the pointer then hands left-subtree values to the right side.

**Key idea:** For each node, compute the best *downward* path starting there (node + the better child, dropping negative children). The best overall path bends at some node: node + left gain + right gain.

**Brute force:** At every node, recompute the best downward gains of both children from scratch and combine (approach 3). Time O(n²) worst case, Space O(h).

:::details 1. Post-order DFS with a global best (optimal)
`gain(node)` returns `node.val + max(leftGain, rightGain)`, where a negative child gain is replaced by 0 (just don't extend that way). While at the node, also try the bent path `node.val + leftGain + rightGain` against the global best. The parent can only use a straight path, which is why it gets the single-branch value.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private int best;

    public int maxPathSum(TreeNode root) {
        best = root.val;
        gain(root);
        return best;
    }

    // best sum of a downward path starting at node
    private int gain(TreeNode node) {
        if (node == null) return 0;
        int left = Math.max(gain(node.left), 0);
        int right = Math.max(gain(node.right), 0);
        best = Math.max(best, node.val + left + right); // path bending at node
        return node.val + Math.max(left, right);
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def maxPathSum(self, root: Optional[TreeNode]) -> int:
        best = root.val

        def gain(node):
            # best sum of a downward path starting at node (never negative)
            nonlocal best
            if not node:
                return 0
            left = max(gain(node.left), 0)
            right = max(gain(node.right), 0)
            best = max(best, node.val + left + right)  # path bending at node
            return node.val + max(left, right)

        gain(root)
        return best
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(h) recursion stack
:::

:::details 2. Iterative (reverse pre-order)
Collect nodes in an order where each parent comes before its children, then process that list backwards so children are always done first. Same formulas, no recursion, so a 3·10⁴-node chain is safe in Python.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int maxPathSum(TreeNode root) {
        // list nodes so that every child comes after its parent, then go backwards
        List<TreeNode> order = new ArrayList<>();
        Deque<TreeNode> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        stack.push(root);
        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode node = stack.pop();
            order.add(node);
            if (node.left != null) stack.push(node.left);
            if (node.right != null) stack.push(node.right);
        }
        Map<TreeNode, Integer> gain = new HashMap<>();
        int best = root.val;
        for (int i = order.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            TreeNode node = order.get(i);
            int left = Math.max(gain.getOrDefault(node.left, 0), 0);
            int right = Math.max(gain.getOrDefault(node.right, 0), 0);
            best = Math.max(best, node.val + left + right);
            gain.put(node, node.val + Math.max(left, right));
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

**Python**

