:::

**Pitfall:** Returning true when the path exists but the last node isn't the end of a word ("ba" should not match after only adding "bad").

**Key idea:** Put all the words in a trie and run one backtracking DFS from every cell, walking the board and the trie together. A path that isn't a prefix of any word is abandoned immediately, and found words are pruned from the trie.

**Brute force:** Run the single-word Word Search backtracking separately for every word (approach 2). Time O(W · m·n · 4·3^(L−1)), Space O(L).

:::details 1. Trie + backtracking with pruning (optimal)
Build a trie and store each word on its last node. From each cell, DFS while the current letters still form a trie path; mark cells as `#` while they're on the path and restore them afterwards. When a node holds a word, record it and clear it so it's reported once. Then prune: in Python delete trie nodes that became empty; in Java keep a count of unfound words per node and skip nodes whose count reaches 0. Pruning is what keeps large word lists fast.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private static class TrieNode {
        TrieNode[] next = new TrieNode[26];
        String word; // set on the node where a word ends
        int count;   // words still unfound below (and at) this node
    }

    private final List<String> res = new ArrayList<>();

    public List<String> findWords(char[][] board, String[] words) {
        TrieNode root = new TrieNode();
        for (String w : words) {
            TrieNode node = root;
            node.count++;
            for (char ch : w.toCharArray()) {
                if (node.next[ch - 'a'] == null) node.next[ch - 'a'] = new TrieNode();
                node = node.next[ch - 'a'];
                node.count++;
            }
            node.word = w;
        }
        for (int r = 0; r < board.length; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < board[0].length; c++) dfs(board, r, c, root);
        return res;
    }

    private int dfs(char[][] board, int r, int c, TrieNode parent) {
        if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= board.length || c >= board[0].length) return 0;
        char ch = board[r][c];
        if (ch == '#') return 0;
        TrieNode node = parent.next[ch - 'a'];
        if (node == null || node.count == 0) return 0;
        int found = 0;
        if (node.word != null) {
            res.add(node.word);
            node.word = null; // report once
            found++;
        }
        board[r][c] = '#';
        found += dfs(board, r + 1, c, node) + dfs(board, r - 1, c, node)
               + dfs(board, r, c + 1, node) + dfs(board, r, c - 1, node);
        board[r][c] = ch;
        node.count -= found; // prune branches whose words are all found
        return found;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def findWords(self, board: List[List[str]], words: List[str]) -> List[str]:
        root = {}
        for w in words:
            node = root
            for ch in w:
                node = node.setdefault(ch, {})
            node["$"] = w  # store the whole word at its end node

        rows, cols, res = len(board), len(board[0]), []

        def dfs(r, c, parent):
            ch = board[r][c]
            node = parent[ch]
            if "$" in node:
                res.append(node.pop("$"))  # report once
            board[r][c] = "#"
            for nr, nc in ((r + 1, c), (r - 1, c), (r, c + 1), (r, c - 1)):
                if 0 <= nr < rows and 0 <= nc < cols and board[nr][nc] in node:
                    dfs(nr, nc, node)
            board[r][c] = ch
            if not node:
                parent.pop(ch)  # prune: no words left below this node

        for r in range(rows):
            for c in range(cols):
                if board[r][c] in root:
                    dfs(r, c, root)
        return res
```

**Time:** O(m·n · 4·3^(L−1)) worst case, L = longest word (10); far less in practice thanks to pruning
**Space:** O(total characters in words) for the trie, O(L) recursion
:::

:::details 2. Brute force: Word Search for each word
For each distinct word, try every starting cell with the classic backtracking search. Repeats the same board exploration for every word, so it times out on big inputs.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<String> findWords(char[][] board, String[] words) {
        List<String> res = new ArrayList<>();
        for (String w : new HashSet<>(Arrays.asList(words))) if (exist(board, w)) res.add(w);
        return res;
    }

    private boolean exist(char[][] board, String w) {
        for (int r = 0; r < board.length; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < board[0].length; c++)
                if (dfs(board, w, r, c, 0)) return true;
        return false;
    }

    private boolean dfs(char[][] board, String w, int r, int c, int i) {
        if (i == w.length()) return true;
        if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= board.length || c >= board[0].length || board[r][c] != w.charAt(i)) return false;
        board[r][c] = '#';
        boolean ok = dfs(board, w, r + 1, c, i + 1) || dfs(board, w, r - 1, c, i + 1)
                  || dfs(board, w, r, c + 1, i + 1) || dfs(board, w, r, c - 1, i + 1);
        board[r][c] = w.charAt(i);
        return ok;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def findWords(self, board: List[List[str]], words: List[str]) -> List[str]:
        rows, cols = len(board), len(board[0])

        def exist(word):
            def dfs(r, c, i):
                if i == len(word):
                    return True
                if not (0 <= r < rows and 0 <= c < cols) or board[r][c] != word[i]:
                    return False
                board[r][c] = "#"
                ok = (dfs(r + 1, c, i + 1) or dfs(r - 1, c, i + 1)
                      or dfs(r, c + 1, i + 1) or dfs(r, c - 1, i + 1))
                board[r][c] = word[i]
                return ok

            return any(dfs(r, c, 0) for r in range(rows) for c in range(cols))

        return [w for w in set(words) if exist(w)]
```

**Time:** O(W · m·n · 4·3^(L−1))
**Space:** O(L) recursion
:::

**Pitfall:** Adding the same word twice when it can be traced from two places. Clear the word from the trie node (or use a set) the first time it's found.

**Key idea:** Keep only the k largest values in a min-heap of size k. Its top (the smallest of those k) is exactly the k-th largest.

**Brute force:** Keep the whole stream, sort after each add and index from the end. Time O(n log n) per add, Space O(n).

:::details 1. Min-heap of size k (optimal)
Push every new value; if the heap grows beyond k, pop the minimum, which can never be the k-th largest again. The constructor just feeds the initial numbers through `add`.

**Java**

```java
class KthLargest {
    private final int k;
    private final PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(); // k largest values seen

    public KthLargest(int k, int[] nums) {
        this.k = k;
        for (int x : nums) add(x);
    }

    public int add(int val) {
        heap.offer(val);
        if (heap.size() > k) heap.poll();
        return heap.peek();
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class KthLargest:
    def __init__(self, k: int, nums: List[int]):
        self.k = k
        self.heap = []  # min-heap holding the k largest values seen
        for x in nums:
            self.add(x)

    def add(self, val: int) -> int:
        heapq.heappush(self.heap, val)
        if len(self.heap) > self.k:
            heapq.heappop(self.heap)
        return self.heap[0]
```

**Time:** O(log k) per add; O(n log k) to build
**Space:** O(k)
:::

:::details 2. Sorted list with binary-search insert
Keep all values sorted. Each add finds the insertion point by binary search and inserts; the answer is the k-th element from the end. Lookup is fast, but inserting shifts elements.

**Java**

```java
class KthLargest {
    private final int k;
    private final List<Integer> nums = new ArrayList<>();

    public KthLargest(int k, int[] nums) {
        this.k = k;
        for (int x : nums) this.nums.add(x);
        Collections.sort(this.nums);
    }

    public int add(int val) {
        int i = Collections.binarySearch(nums, val);
        nums.add(i < 0 ? -i - 1 : i, val); // keep the list sorted
        return nums.get(nums.size() - k);
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class KthLargest:
    def __init__(self, k: int, nums: List[int]):
        self.k = k
        self.nums = sorted(nums)

    def add(self, val: int) -> int:
        bisect.insort(self.nums, val)  # keep the list sorted
        return self.nums[-self.k]
```

**Time:** O(n) per add (shifting), O(n log n) to build
**Space:** O(n)
:::

**Pitfall:** Using a max-heap of everything and popping k times per query: correct but O(k log n) per add and it destroys the heap unless you push the items back.

**Key idea:** You always need the two largest values, then insert one new value: that's exactly what a max-heap does in O(log n).

**Brute force:** Re-sort the array before every smash and take the last two. Time O(n² log n), Space O(1) extra.

:::details 1. Max-heap (standard)
Put all stones in a max-heap (Python: negate the values in `heapq`). Pop two, push the difference if it's non-zero, repeat until at most one stone remains.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int lastStoneWeight(int[] stones) {
        PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());
        for (int s : stones) heap.offer(s);
        while (heap.size() > 1) {
            int y = heap.poll(), x = heap.poll();
            if (y > x) heap.offer(y - x);
        }
        return heap.isEmpty() ? 0 : heap.peek();
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def lastStoneWeight(self, stones: List[int]) -> int:
        heap = [-s for s in stones]  # negate: heapq is a min-heap
        heapq.heapify(heap)
        while len(heap) > 1:
            y = -heapq.heappop(heap)
            x = -heapq.heappop(heap)
            if y > x:
                heapq.heappush(heap, -(y - x))
        return -heap[0] if heap else 0
```

**Time:** O(n log n)
**Space:** O(n)
:::

:::details 2. Sorted list + binary insertion
Sort once, pop the two largest from the end, and insert the difference back at its sorted position with binary search. Fine for n ≤ 30.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int lastStoneWeight(int[] stones) {
        List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int s : stones) list.add(s);
        Collections.sort(list);
        while (list.size() > 1) {
            int y = list.remove(list.size() - 1), x = list.remove(list.size() - 1);
            if (y > x) {
                int i = Collections.binarySearch(list, y - x);
                list.add(i < 0 ? -i - 1 : i, y - x);
            }
        }
        return list.isEmpty() ? 0 : list.get(0);
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def lastStoneWeight(self, stones: List[int]) -> int:
        stones = sorted(stones)
        while len(stones) > 1:
            y = stones.pop()
            x = stones.pop()
            if y > x:
                bisect.insort(stones, y - x)
        return stones[0] if stones else 0
```

**Time:** O(n²) (each insert shifts up to n elements)
**Space:** O(n)
:::

:::details 3. Bucket counts (weights are small)
Count stones by weight (≤ 1000) and sweep from the heaviest down, carrying the one stone (`first`) still waiting for a partner. Equal pairs cancel immediately (`count %= 2`). A smash leaves `first − w`; if that is at least `w` it is still the heaviest stone and stays as `first`, otherwise it goes back into its bucket.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int lastStoneWeight(int[] stones) {
        int max = 0;
        for (int s : stones) max = Math.max(max, s);
        int[] count = new int[max + 1];
        for (int s : stones) count[s]++;
        int w = max, first = 0; // first = heaviest stone waiting for a partner
        while (w > 0) {
            if (count[w] == 0) { w--; continue; }
            if (first == 0) {
                count[w] %= 2; // equal pairs destroy each other
                if (count[w] == 1) { first = w; count[w] = 0; }
                w--;
            } else {
                count[w]--;
                int diff = first - w;
                first = 0;
                if (diff > 0) {
                    if (diff >= w) first = diff; // the remainder is at least as heavy as w
                    else count[diff]++;
                }
            }
        }
        return first;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def lastStoneWeight(self, stones: List[int]) -> int:
        count = [0] * (max(stones) + 1)
        for s in stones:
            count[s] += 1
        w, first = len(count) - 1, 0  # first = heaviest stone waiting for a partner
        while w > 0:
            if count[w] == 0:
                w -= 1
                continue
            if first == 0:
                count[w] %= 2  # equal pairs destroy each other
                if count[w]:
                    first, count[w] = w, 0
                w -= 1
            else:
                count[w] -= 1
                diff = first - w
                first = 0
                if diff:
                    if diff >= w:  # the remainder is at least as heavy as w
                        first = diff
                    else:
                        count[diff] += 1
        return first
```

**Time:** O(n + W), W = max weight
**Space:** O(W)
:::

**Pitfall:** Pushing a zero-weight stone back when the two are equal. It then gets "smashed" later and can turn a final answer of 0 into a wrong one.

**Key idea:** Compare squared distances `x² + y²` (no square root needed) and keep only the k smallest: a size-k max-heap, or Quickselect to partition the k nearest to the front.

**Brute force:** Sort all points by squared distance and take the first k (approach 3). Time O(n log n), Space O(n) for the sort.

:::details 1. Max-heap of size k
Push each point keyed by its distance; when the heap has more than k points, pop the farthest. What remains are the k closest. Python's `heapq` is a min-heap, so store negated distances.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int[][] kClosest(int[][] points, int k) {
        // max-heap by distance, holding the k closest so far
        PriorityQueue<int[]> heap = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> dist(b) - dist(a));
        for (int[] p : points) {
            heap.offer(p);
            if (heap.size() > k) heap.poll();
        }
        return heap.toArray(new int[0][]);
    }

    private int dist(int[] p) {
        return p[0] * p[0] + p[1] * p[1];
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def kClosest(self, points: List[List[int]], k: int) -> List[List[int]]:
        heap = []  # max-heap (negated distance) of the k closest so far
        for x, y in points:
            heapq.heappush(heap, (-(x * x + y * y), x, y))
            if len(heap) > k:
                heapq.heappop(heap)
        return [[x, y] for _, x, y in heap]
```

**Time:** O(n log k)
**Space:** O(k)
:::

:::details 2. Quickselect (average linear)
Partition around a random pivot distance: closer points to the left, the pivot at index `store`. If `store == k − 1`, the first k points are the answer; otherwise keep partitioning only the side that contains index k − 1.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private final Random rnd = new Random();

    public int[][] kClosest(int[][] points, int k) {
        int lo = 0, hi = points.length - 1;
        while (lo < hi) {
            // Lomuto partition around a random pivot
            swap(points, lo + rnd.nextInt(hi - lo + 1), hi);
            int pivot = dist(points[hi]), store = lo;
            for (int i = lo; i < hi; i++)
                if (dist(points[i]) < pivot) swap(points, i, store++);
            swap(points, store, hi);
            if (store == k - 1) break;
            if (store < k - 1) lo = store + 1;
            else hi = store - 1;
        }
        return Arrays.copyOf(points, k);
    }

    private int dist(int[] p) {
        return p[0] * p[0] + p[1] * p[1];
    }

    private void swap(int[][] a, int i, int j) {
        int[] t = a[i]; a[i] = a[j]; a[j] = t;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def kClosest(self, points: List[List[int]], k: int) -> List[List[int]]:
        dist = lambda p: p[0] * p[0] + p[1] * p[1]
        lo, hi = 0, len(points) - 1
        while lo < hi:
            # Lomuto partition around a random pivot
            pi = random.randint(lo, hi)
            points[pi], points[hi] = points[hi], points[pi]
            pivot, store = dist(points[hi]), lo
            for i in range(lo, hi):
                if dist(points[i]) < pivot:
                    points[i], points[store] = points[store], points[i]
                    store += 1
            points[store], points[hi] = points[hi], points[store]
            if store == k - 1:
                break
            if store < k - 1:
                lo = store + 1
            else:
                hi = store - 1
        return points[:k]
```

**Time:** O(n) average, O(n²) worst case (made unlikely by the random pivot)
**Space:** O(1) extra, in place
:::

:::details 3. Sort by distance
Sort every point by `x² + y²` and return the first k. Shortest to write and fast enough for n = 10⁴.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int[][] kClosest(int[][] points, int k) {
        int[][] sorted = points.clone();
        Arrays.sort(sorted, (a, b) -> (a[0] * a[0] + a[1] * a[1]) - (b[0] * b[0] + b[1] * b[1]));
        return Arrays.copyOf(sorted, k);
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def kClosest(self, points: List[List[int]], k: int) -> List[List[int]]:
        return sorted(points, key=lambda p: p[0] * p[0] + p[1] * p[1])[:k]
```

**Time:** O(n log n)
**Space:** O(n) (Python's sort / the copied array)
:::

**Pitfall:** Taking `sqrt` of the distances. It's unnecessary and floating-point rounding can break ties wrongly; compare the integer `x² + y²` instead.

**Key idea:** You don't need the full order, only the boundary: a size-k min-heap keeps the k largest, and Quickselect partitions until the pivot lands at index n − k.

**Brute force:** Sort and return `nums[n − k]` (approach 4). Time O(n log n), Space O(1)–O(n) depending on the sort.

:::details 1. Min-heap of size k
Push each number; if the heap exceeds k elements, pop the minimum. The heap then holds the k largest values, and its top is the k-th largest.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int findKthLargest(int[] nums, int k) {
        PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(); // k largest so far
        for (int x : nums) {
            heap.offer(x);
            if (heap.size() > k) heap.poll();
        }
        return heap.peek();
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def findKthLargest(self, nums: List[int], k: int) -> int:
        heap = []  # min-heap of the k largest so far
        for x in nums:
            heapq.heappush(heap, x)
            if len(heap) > k:
                heapq.heappop(heap)
        return heap[0]
```

**Time:** O(n log k)
**Space:** O(k)
:::

:::details 2. Quickselect with 3-way partition
The answer sits at index `n − k` of the ascending order. Pick a random pivot and partition into `< pivot`, `== pivot`, `> pivot`. If the target index falls in the middle block, the pivot is the answer; otherwise continue only on the side that contains it. The 3-way split keeps arrays full of duplicates from degrading to O(n²).

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private final Random rnd = new Random();

    public int findKthLargest(int[] nums, int k) {
        int target = nums.length - k; // index of the answer in ascending order
        int lo = 0, hi = nums.length - 1;
        while (true) {
            int pivot = nums[lo + rnd.nextInt(hi - lo + 1)];
            // 3-way partition: [< pivot][== pivot][> pivot]
            int lt = lo, i = lo, gt = hi;
            while (i <= gt) {
                if (nums[i] < pivot) swap(nums, lt++, i++);
                else if (nums[i] > pivot) swap(nums, gt--, i);
                else i++;
            }
            if (target < lt) hi = lt - 1;
            else if (target > gt) lo = gt + 1;
            else return pivot;
        }
    }

    private void swap(int[] a, int i, int j) {
        int t = a[i]; a[i] = a[j]; a[j] = t;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def findKthLargest(self, nums: List[int], k: int) -> int:
        target = len(nums) - k  # index of the answer in ascending order
        lo, hi = 0, len(nums) - 1
        while True:
            pivot = nums[random.randint(lo, hi)]
            # 3-way partition: [< pivot][== pivot][> pivot]
            lt, i, gt = lo, lo, hi
            while i <= gt:
                if nums[i] < pivot:
                    nums[lt], nums[i] = nums[i], nums[lt]
                    lt += 1
                    i += 1
                elif nums[i] > pivot:
                    nums[gt], nums[i] = nums[i], nums[gt]
                    gt -= 1
                else:
                    i += 1
            if target < lt:
                hi = lt - 1
            elif target > gt:
                lo = gt + 1
            else:
                return pivot
```

**Time:** O(n) average, O(n²) worst case (unlikely with random pivots)
**Space:** O(1), in place
:::

:::details 3. Counting sort (values are bounded)
Values lie in [−10⁴, 10⁴], so count how often each value occurs, then walk the counts from the top, subtracting from k until it reaches 0.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int findKthLargest(int[] nums, int k) {
        int lo = Integer.MAX_VALUE, hi = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        for (int x : nums) { lo = Math.min(lo, x); hi = Math.max(hi, x); }
        int[] count = new int[hi - lo + 1];
        for (int x : nums) count[x - lo]++;
        for (int v = count.length - 1; v >= 0; v--) {
            k -= count[v];
            if (k <= 0) return v + lo;
        }
        return -1;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def findKthLargest(self, nums: List[int], k: int) -> int:
        lo = min(nums)
        count = [0] * (max(nums) - lo + 1)
        for x in nums:
            count[x - lo] += 1
        for v in range(len(count) - 1, -1, -1):
            k -= count[v]
            if k <= 0:
                return v + lo
        return -1
```

**Time:** O(n + R), R = value range (2·10⁴ + 1)
**Space:** O(R)
:::

:::details 4. Sort
Sort ascending and read index `n − k`.

**Java**

