    def __init__(self):
        self.nums = []  # kept sorted

    def addNum(self, num: int) -> None:
        bisect.insort(self.nums, num)

    def findMedian(self) -> float:
        n = len(self.nums)
        if n % 2:
            return float(self.nums[n // 2])
        return (self.nums[n // 2 - 1] + self.nums[n // 2]) / 2
```

**Time:** O(n) per add, O(1) per median
**Space:** O(n)
:::

**Pitfall:** Integer division when averaging the two middle values (`(a + b) / 2` with ints in Java truncates 1.5 to 1). Cast to `double` first; this also avoids overflow.

**Key idea:** Every element makes one binary choice, in or out. Walking those choices (recursion, doubling a list, or bitmasks) produces each of the 2ⁿ subsets exactly once.

**Brute force:** Generating subsets is already exponential, and any method must output 2ⁿ lists of up to n values. A naive variant generates all n! orderings of every prefix and deduplicates with a set: far worse, O(n!·n).

:::details 1. Backtracking: include / exclude
`dfs(i)` decides element `i`: append it and recurse, then pop it and recurse again without it. When `i == n`, copy the current list into the result. The recursion tree has 2ⁿ leaves, one per subset.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> subsets(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        dfs(nums, 0, new ArrayList<>(), res);
        return res;
    }

    private void dfs(int[] nums, int i, List<Integer> cur, List<List<Integer>> res) {
        if (i == nums.length) {
            res.add(new ArrayList<>(cur));
            return;
        }
        cur.add(nums[i]);            // take nums[i]
        dfs(nums, i + 1, cur, res);
        cur.remove(cur.size() - 1);  // skip nums[i]
        dfs(nums, i + 1, cur, res);
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def subsets(self, nums: List[int]) -> List[List[int]]:
        res, cur = [], []

        def dfs(i):
            if i == len(nums):
                res.append(cur.copy())
                return
            cur.append(nums[i])  # take nums[i]
            dfs(i + 1)
            cur.pop()            # skip nums[i]
            dfs(i + 1)

        dfs(0)
        return res
```

**Time:** O(n · 2ⁿ), 2ⁿ subsets, O(n) to copy each
**Space:** O(n) recursion and current list (output excluded)
:::

:::details 2. Iterative doubling
Start with `[[]]`. For each number, copy every subset you have so far and add the number to the copy. After processing all n numbers the list has doubled n times.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> subsets(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        res.add(new ArrayList<>());
        for (int x : nums) {
            int size = res.size();
            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) { // every old subset, with x added
                List<Integer> s = new ArrayList<>(res.get(i));
                s.add(x);
                res.add(s);
            }
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def subsets(self, nums: List[int]) -> List[List[int]]:
        res = [[]]
        for x in nums:
            res += [s + [x] for s in res]  # every old subset, with x added
        return res
```

**Time:** O(n · 2ⁿ)
**Space:** O(1) besides the output
:::

:::details 3. Bitmask enumeration
Count `mask` from 0 to 2ⁿ − 1; bit `i` of the mask says whether `nums[i]` is in the subset. Neat and non-recursive.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> subsets(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int mask = 0; mask < (1 << n); mask++) {
            List<Integer> s = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if ((mask >> i & 1) == 1) s.add(nums[i]);
            res.add(s);
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def subsets(self, nums: List[int]) -> List[List[int]]:
        n = len(nums)
        return [[nums[i] for i in range(n) if mask >> i & 1] for mask in range(1 << n)]
```

**Time:** O(n · 2ⁿ)
**Space:** O(1) besides the output
:::

**Pitfall:** Appending `cur` itself instead of a copy. Every entry then points at the same list, which ends up empty after backtracking.

**Key idea:** Backtrack over candidates in index order. After choosing `candidates[i]`, recurse from `i` again (reuse allowed) but never from an earlier index, so each multiset is built in exactly one order.

**Brute force:** Recurse trying every candidate at every step (any order), collect sorted combinations into a set to remove permutations of the same multiset. Exponentially more branches than needed. Time O(N^(T/m)) with duplicates, Space O(T/m) depth.

:::details 1. Backtracking with a start index (sorted, with pruning)
Sort the candidates. `dfs(start, remain)` tries each `i ≥ start`; once `candidates[i] > remain` it stops the loop, since every later value is bigger. Recursing with `i` (not `i + 1`) allows reuse.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> combinationSum(int[] candidates, int target) {
        Arrays.sort(candidates);
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        dfs(candidates, 0, target, new ArrayList<>(), res);
        return res;
    }

    private void dfs(int[] c, int start, int remain, List<Integer> cur, List<List<Integer>> res) {
        if (remain == 0) {
            res.add(new ArrayList<>(cur));
            return;
        }
        for (int i = start; i < c.length && c[i] <= remain; i++) { // sorted: stop at the first too-big value
            cur.add(c[i]);
            dfs(c, i, remain - c[i], cur, res); // i, not i + 1: reuse allowed
            cur.remove(cur.size() - 1);
        }
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def combinationSum(self, candidates: List[int], target: int) -> List[List[int]]:
        candidates.sort()
        res, cur = [], []

        def dfs(start, remain):
            if remain == 0:
                res.append(cur.copy())
                return
            for i in range(start, len(candidates)):
                if candidates[i] > remain:
                    break  # sorted: every later candidate is too big too
                cur.append(candidates[i])
                dfs(i, remain - candidates[i])  # i, not i + 1: reuse allowed
                cur.pop()

        dfs(0, target)
        return res
```

**Time:** O(N^(T/m + 1)) worst case, T = target, m = smallest candidate (the depth is at most T/m)
**Space:** O(T/m) recursion depth (output excluded)
:::

:::details 2. Binary decision tree: use it again or move on
At index `i`, either take `candidates[i]` and stay at `i`, or skip to `i + 1` forever. Same search space, written as two branches instead of a loop.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> combinationSum(int[] candidates, int target) {
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        dfs(candidates, 0, target, new ArrayList<>(), res);
        return res;
    }

    private void dfs(int[] c, int i, int remain, List<Integer> cur, List<List<Integer>> res) {
        if (remain == 0) {
            res.add(new ArrayList<>(cur));
            return;
        }
        if (i == c.length || remain < 0) return;
        cur.add(c[i]);                 // use c[i] (again)
        dfs(c, i, remain - c[i], cur, res);
        cur.remove(cur.size() - 1);
        dfs(c, i + 1, remain, cur, res); // move past c[i] for good
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
