:::

:::details 3. DP over targets (unbounded knapsack style)
`dp[t]` is the list of combinations that sum to `t`. Process candidates in the outer loop (so each combination is built in candidate order, never permuted), and extend every combination in `dp[t − c]` with `c`.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> combinationSum(int[] candidates, int target) {
        // dp[t] = all combinations summing to t, built candidate by candidate
        List<List<List<Integer>>> dp = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int t = 0; t <= target; t++) dp.add(new ArrayList<>());
        dp.get(0).add(new ArrayList<>());
        for (int c : candidates) {
            for (int t = c; t <= target; t++) {
                for (List<Integer> combo : dp.get(t - c)) {
                    List<Integer> next = new ArrayList<>(combo);
                    next.add(c);
                    dp.get(t).add(next);
                }
            }
        }
        return dp.get(target);
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def combinationSum(self, candidates: List[int], target: int) -> List[List[int]]:
        # dp[t] = all combinations summing to t, built candidate by candidate
        dp = [[] for _ in range(target + 1)]
        dp[0] = [[]]
        for c in candidates:
            for t in range(c, target + 1):
                for combo in dp[t - c]:
                    dp[t].append(combo + [c])
        return dp[target]
```

**Time:** O(N · T · K), K = combinations stored per sum (each copied when extended)
**Space:** O(total size of all stored combinations)
:::

**Pitfall:** Recursing from 0 on every call. You then get `[2,2,3]`, `[2,3,2]` and `[3,2,2]` as three separate answers.

**Key idea:** Sort, then backtrack from index `i + 1` (each element once). At any one position of the combination, try each distinct value only once: skip `candidates[i]` if it equals `candidates[i−1]` and `i > start`.

**Brute force:** Enumerate all 2ⁿ take/skip choices and dedupe the hits through a set of sorted tuples (approach 3). Time O(n · 2ⁿ), Space O(n · 2ⁿ) worst case.

:::details 1. Sorted backtracking with a duplicate skip
`dfs(start, remain)` loops `i` from `start`. Skip a value equal to the previous one at the same level (it would only repeat an earlier branch), stop once a value exceeds `remain` (sorted), and recurse with `i + 1`.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> combinationSum2(int[] candidates, int target) {
        Arrays.sort(candidates);
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        dfs(candidates, 0, target, new ArrayList<>(), res);
        return res;
    }

    private void dfs(int[] c, int start, int remain, List<Integer> cur, List<List<Integer>> res) {
        if (remain == 0) {
            res.add(new ArrayList<>(cur));
            return;
        }
        for (int i = start; i < c.length && c[i] <= remain; i++) {
            if (i > start && c[i] == c[i - 1]) continue; // same value already tried at this position
            cur.add(c[i]);
            dfs(c, i + 1, remain - c[i], cur, res); // i + 1: each element once
            cur.remove(cur.size() - 1);
        }
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def combinationSum2(self, candidates: List[int], target: int) -> List[List[int]]:
        candidates.sort()
        res, cur = [], []

        def dfs(start, remain):
            if remain == 0:
                res.append(cur.copy())
                return
            for i in range(start, len(candidates)):
                if i > start and candidates[i] == candidates[i - 1]:
                    continue  # same value already tried at this position
                if candidates[i] > remain:
                    break
                cur.append(candidates[i])
                dfs(i + 1, remain - candidates[i])  # i + 1: each element once
                cur.pop()

        dfs(0, target)
        return res
```

**Time:** O(n · 2ⁿ) worst case; pruning by `remain ≤ 30` makes it far smaller
**Space:** O(n) recursion (output excluded)
:::

:::details 2. Group by value, choose how many copies
Count each distinct value. For each value decide to use 0, 1, …, `count` copies, then move to the next value. Duplicates can't arise because each value is decided exactly once.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> combinationSum2(int[] candidates, int target) {
        TreeMap<Integer, Integer> count = new TreeMap<>();
        for (int c : candidates) count.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
        int[][] items = new int[count.size()][]; // {value, how many copies}
        int k = 0;
        for (Map.Entry<Integer, Integer> e : count.entrySet()) items[k++] = new int[]{e.getKey(), e.getValue()};
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        dfs(items, 0, target, new ArrayList<>(), res);
        return res;
    }

    private void dfs(int[][] items, int i, int remain, List<Integer> cur, List<List<Integer>> res) {
        if (remain == 0) {
            res.add(new ArrayList<>(cur));
            return;
        }
        if (i == items.length || items[i][0] > remain) return;
        int val = items[i][0], cnt = items[i][1];
        // use 0, 1, ..., cnt copies of val (as long as they fit)
        dfs(items, i + 1, remain, cur, res);
        int used = 0;
        while (used < cnt && remain >= val) {
            cur.add(val);
            used++;
            remain -= val;
            dfs(items, i + 1, remain, cur, res);
        }
        for (int j = 0; j < used; j++) cur.remove(cur.size() - 1);
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def combinationSum2(self, candidates: List[int], target: int) -> List[List[int]]:
        items = sorted(Counter(candidates).items())  # (value, how many copies)
        res, cur = [], []

        def dfs(i, remain):
            if remain == 0:
                res.append(cur.copy())
                return
            if i == len(items) or items[i][0] > remain:
                return
            val, cnt = items[i]
            # use 0, 1, ..., cnt copies of val (as long as they fit)
            dfs(i + 1, remain)
            used = 0
            while used < cnt and remain >= val:
                cur.append(val)
                used += 1
                remain -= val
                dfs(i + 1, remain)
            del cur[len(cur) - used:]

        dfs(0, target)
        return res
```

**Time:** O(∏(countᵢ + 1)) branches, bounded by 2ⁿ, cut down by `remain`
**Space:** O(n)
:::

:::details 3. Brute force: take/skip + set
Classic subset recursion on the sorted array, with every exact hit stored as a tuple in a set so repeated combinations collapse.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> combinationSum2(int[] candidates, int target) {
        Arrays.sort(candidates);
        Set<List<Integer>> found = new HashSet<>();
        dfs(candidates, 0, target, new ArrayList<>(), found);
        return new ArrayList<>(found);
    }

    private void dfs(int[] c, int i, int remain, List<Integer> cur, Set<List<Integer>> found) {
        if (remain == 0) {
            found.add(new ArrayList<>(cur));
            return;
        }
        if (i == c.length || remain < 0) return;
        cur.add(c[i]);                        // take
        dfs(c, i + 1, remain - c[i], cur, found);
        cur.remove(cur.size() - 1);
        dfs(c, i + 1, remain, cur, found);    // skip
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def combinationSum2(self, candidates: List[int], target: int) -> List[List[int]]:
        candidates.sort()
        found = set()

        def dfs(i, remain, cur):
            if remain == 0:
                found.add(tuple(cur))
                return
            if i == len(candidates) or remain < 0:
                return
            dfs(i + 1, remain - candidates[i], cur + [candidates[i]])  # take
            dfs(i + 1, remain, cur)                                     # skip

        dfs(0, target, [])
        return [list(t) for t in found]
```

**Time:** O(n · 2ⁿ) worst case (pruned by `remain < 0`)
**Space:** O(n · 2ⁿ) worst case for the set
:::

**Pitfall:** Skipping duplicates with `i > 0` instead of `i > start`. That forbids using the second 1 even after the first one is in the combination, so `[1,1,6]` disappears.

**Key idea:** Fill positions one at a time; each position can take any element not used yet. Backtracking over "used" choices visits every one of the n! orderings exactly once.

**Brute force:** Generate all nⁿ sequences of length n (any element at every position) and keep those with no repeated element. Time O(n · nⁿ), Space O(n).

:::details 1. Backtracking with a used[] array
Build the permutation in `cur`. Loop over all elements; if one isn't used, mark it, append it, recurse, then undo both. When `cur` has n elements, copy it out.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        dfs(nums, new boolean[nums.length], new ArrayList<>(), res);
        return res;
    }

    private void dfs(int[] nums, boolean[] used, List<Integer> cur, List<List<Integer>> res) {
        if (cur.size() == nums.length) {
            res.add(new ArrayList<>(cur));
            return;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (used[i]) continue;
            used[i] = true;
            cur.add(nums[i]);
            dfs(nums, used, cur, res);
            cur.remove(cur.size() - 1);
            used[i] = false;
        }
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def permute(self, nums: List[int]) -> List[List[int]]:
        res, cur = [], []
        used = [False] * len(nums)

        def dfs():
            if len(cur) == len(nums):
                res.append(cur.copy())
                return
            for i, x in enumerate(nums):
                if not used[i]:
                    used[i] = True
                    cur.append(x)
                    dfs()
                    cur.pop()
                    used[i] = False

        dfs()
        return res
```

**Time:** O(n · n!), n! leaves, O(n) to copy each
**Space:** O(n) for recursion, `cur` and `used`
:::

:::details 2. In-place swapping
Positions `0..i−1` are fixed. For position `i`, swap each `nums[j]` (j ≥ i) into it, recurse on `i + 1`, then swap back. No extra `used` array is needed.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        dfs(nums, 0, res);
        return res;
    }

    private void dfs(int[] nums, int i, List<List<Integer>> res) {
        if (i == nums.length) {
            List<Integer> p = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int x : nums) p.add(x);
            res.add(p);
            return;
        }
        for (int j = i; j < nums.length; j++) {
            swap(nums, i, j); // put nums[j] at position i
            dfs(nums, i + 1, res);
            swap(nums, i, j); // undo
        }
    }

    private void swap(int[] a, int i, int j) {
        int t = a[i]; a[i] = a[j]; a[j] = t;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def permute(self, nums: List[int]) -> List[List[int]]:
        res = []

        def dfs(i):
            if i == len(nums):
                res.append(nums.copy())
                return
            for j in range(i, len(nums)):
                nums[i], nums[j] = nums[j], nums[i]  # put nums[j] at position i
                dfs(i + 1)
                nums[i], nums[j] = nums[j], nums[i]  # undo

        dfs(0)
        return res
```

**Time:** O(n · n!)
**Space:** O(n) recursion
:::

:::details 3. Iterative insertion
Start with `[[]]`. For each new number, insert it into every gap of every permutation built so far: a permutation of length k has k + 1 gaps.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> perms = new ArrayList<>();
        perms.add(new ArrayList<>());
        for (int x : nums) {
            List<List<Integer>> next = new ArrayList<>();
            for (List<Integer> p : perms) {
                for (int k = 0; k <= p.size(); k++) { // insert x into every gap
                    List<Integer> q = new ArrayList<>(p);
                    q.add(k, x);
                    next.add(q);
                }
            }
            perms = next;
        }
        return perms;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def permute(self, nums: List[int]) -> List[List[int]]:
        perms = [[]]
        for x in nums:
            # insert x into every gap of every permutation built so far
            perms = [p[:k] + [x] + p[k:] for p in perms for k in range(len(p) + 1)]
        return perms
```

**Time:** O(n · n!)
**Space:** O(n · n!) for the intermediate lists
:::

**Pitfall:** Forgetting to undo the choice (`used[i] = False` / swap back) after the recursive call, which corrupts every later branch.

**Key idea:** Sort so equal values sit together. At each position of the subset being built, use each distinct value at most once: skip `nums[i]` when it equals `nums[i−1]` and `i` isn't the first option at this level.

**Brute force:** Generate all 2ⁿ subsets of the sorted array and deduplicate them with a hash set (approach 3). Time O(n · 2ⁿ), Space O(n · 2ⁿ) for the set.

:::details 1. Backtracking with a skip rule
After sorting, `dfs(start)` records the current subset, then tries each `i ≥ start` as the next element. If `nums[i] == nums[i−1]` and `i > start`, choosing it here would rebuild a subset already produced by the earlier copy, so skip it. Using a duplicate deeper (`i == start`) is still allowed, which is how `[2,2]` appears.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> subsetsWithDup(int[] nums) {
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        dfs(nums, 0, new ArrayList<>(), res);
        return res;
    }

    private void dfs(int[] nums, int start, List<Integer> cur, List<List<Integer>> res) {
        res.add(new ArrayList<>(cur));
        for (int i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (i > start && nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) continue; // same value already tried in this position
            cur.add(nums[i]);
            dfs(nums, i + 1, cur, res);
            cur.remove(cur.size() - 1);
        }
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def subsetsWithDup(self, nums: List[int]) -> List[List[int]]:
        nums.sort()
        res, cur = [], []

        def dfs(start):
            res.append(cur.copy())
            for i in range(start, len(nums)):
                if i > start and nums[i] == nums[i - 1]:
                    continue  # same value already tried in this position
                cur.append(nums[i])
                dfs(i + 1)
                cur.pop()

        dfs(0)
        return res
```

**Time:** O(n · 2ⁿ)
**Space:** O(n) recursion (output excluded)
:::

:::details 2. Iterative doubling, extending only new subsets for repeats
Like the Subsets doubling trick, but when the value repeats the previous one, only extend the subsets created in the previous round (those already containing the earlier copy). Extending older subsets again would create duplicates.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> subsetsWithDup(int[] nums) {
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        res.add(new ArrayList<>());
        int prevEnd = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            // a repeated value only extends the subsets created in the previous round
            int start = (i > 0 && nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) ? prevEnd : 0;
            prevEnd = res.size();
            for (int j = start; j < prevEnd; j++) {
                List<Integer> s = new ArrayList<>(res.get(j));
                s.add(nums[i]);
                res.add(s);
            }
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def subsetsWithDup(self, nums: List[int]) -> List[List[int]]:
        nums.sort()
        res = [[]]
        prev_end = 0
        for i, x in enumerate(nums):
            # a repeated value only extends the subsets created in the previous round
            start = prev_end if i > 0 and x == nums[i - 1] else 0
            prev_end = len(res)
            res += [s + [x] for s in res[start:prev_end]]
        return res
```

**Time:** O(n · 2ⁿ)
**Space:** O(1) besides the output
:::

:::details 3. Brute force: bitmasks + set
Sort, enumerate all 2ⁿ masks, and add each subset (as a tuple / list) to a set so duplicates collapse.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> subsetsWithDup(int[] nums) {
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        Set<List<Integer>> seen = new HashSet<>();
        for (int mask = 0; mask < (1 << nums.length); mask++) {
            List<Integer> s = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) if ((mask >> i & 1) == 1) s.add(nums[i]);
            seen.add(s);
        }
        return new ArrayList<>(seen);
    }
}
```

**Python**

