:::

**Pitfall:** Forgetting to sort first. The skip rule `nums[i] == nums[i−1]` only works when duplicates are adjacent.

**Key idea:** Try every cell as a start and backtrack: match one letter, mark the cell as used, explore the 4 neighbours for the next letter, then unmark it on the way back.

**Brute force:** Enumerate every simple path of length L in the grid (any cell sequence with adjacent steps and no repeats) and compare its letters to the word. That is the same search without the early "letter doesn't match" cut-off. Time O(m·n·4^L), Space O(L).

:::details 1. Backtracking DFS (mark in place)
`dfs(r, c, i)` fails if the cell is off-grid or doesn't hold `word[i]`. Otherwise overwrite the cell with `#` so it can't be reused on this path, try the four neighbours for `i + 1`, then restore the letter. Reaching `i == len(word)` means the whole word matched.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean exist(char[][] board, String word) {
        for (int r = 0; r < board.length; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < board[0].length; c++)
                if (dfs(board, word, r, c, 0)) return true;
        return false;
    }

    private boolean dfs(char[][] board, String word, int r, int c, int i) {
        if (i == word.length()) return true;
        if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= board.length || c >= board[0].length || board[r][c] != word.charAt(i)) return false;
        board[r][c] = '#'; // mark as used on this path
        boolean found = dfs(board, word, r + 1, c, i + 1) || dfs(board, word, r - 1, c, i + 1)
                     || dfs(board, word, r, c + 1, i + 1) || dfs(board, word, r, c - 1, i + 1);
        board[r][c] = word.charAt(i); // restore
        return found;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def exist(self, board: List[List[str]], word: str) -> bool:
        rows, cols = len(board), len(board[0])

        def dfs(r, c, i):
            if i == len(word):
                return True
            if r < 0 or c < 0 or r >= rows or c >= cols or board[r][c] != word[i]:
                return False
            board[r][c] = "#"  # mark as used on this path
            found = (dfs(r + 1, c, i + 1) or dfs(r - 1, c, i + 1) or
                     dfs(r, c + 1, i + 1) or dfs(r, c - 1, i + 1))
            board[r][c] = word[i]  # restore
            return found

        return any(dfs(r, c, 0) for r in range(rows) for c in range(cols))
```

**Time:** O(m·n·3^L): after the first step each cell has at most 3 unused neighbours
**Space:** O(L) recursion depth
:::

:::details 2. Backtracking with pruning
Two cheap checks before searching: if the board doesn't have enough copies of some letter, return false at once. And if the word's first letter is more common on the board than its last, search for the reversed word instead, so there are fewer starting cells. Uses a separate `visited` array so the board stays untouched.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean exist(char[][] board, String word) {
        int[] have = new int[128], need = new int[128];
        for (char[] row : board) for (char ch : row) have[ch]++;
        for (char ch : word.toCharArray()) need[ch]++;
        for (int ch = 0; ch < 128; ch++) if (need[ch] > have[ch]) return false; // letter missing
        if (have[word.charAt(0)] > have[word.charAt(word.length() - 1)])
            word = new StringBuilder(word).reverse().toString(); // start from the rarer end
        boolean[][] visited = new boolean[board.length][board[0].length];
        for (int r = 0; r < board.length; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < board[0].length; c++)
                if (dfs(board, word, visited, r, c, 0)) return true;
        return false;
    }

    private boolean dfs(char[][] b, String w, boolean[][] vis, int r, int c, int i) {
        if (i == w.length()) return true;
        if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= b.length || c >= b[0].length || vis[r][c] || b[r][c] != w.charAt(i)) return false;
        vis[r][c] = true;
        boolean found = dfs(b, w, vis, r + 1, c, i + 1) || dfs(b, w, vis, r - 1, c, i + 1)
                     || dfs(b, w, vis, r, c + 1, i + 1) || dfs(b, w, vis, r, c - 1, i + 1);
        vis[r][c] = false;
        return found;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def exist(self, board: List[List[str]], word: str) -> bool:
        rows, cols = len(board), len(board[0])
        have = Counter(ch for row in board for ch in row)
        need = Counter(word)
        if any(have[ch] < k for ch, k in need.items()):
            return False  # some letter is missing from the board
        if have[word[0]] > have[word[-1]]:
            word = word[::-1]  # start from the rarer end: fewer starting points
        visited = set()

        def dfs(r, c, i):
            if i == len(word):
                return True
            if (r < 0 or c < 0 or r >= rows or c >= cols
                    or (r, c) in visited or board[r][c] != word[i]):
                return False
            visited.add((r, c))
            found = any(dfs(r + dr, c + dc, i + 1) for dr, dc in ((1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1)))
            visited.discard((r, c))
            return found

        return any(dfs(r, c, 0) for r in range(rows) for c in range(cols))
```

**Time:** O(m·n·3^L) worst case, much less in practice
**Space:** O(m·n) for `visited`, O(L) recursion
:::

**Pitfall:** Forgetting to restore the cell after exploring. The next starting cell then sees a board with `#` holes and misses valid paths.

**Key idea:** Backtrack on the end of the next piece: from position `start`, try every `end` such that `s[start..end]` is a palindrome, take that piece and recurse from `end + 1`.

**Brute force:** Try all 2ⁿ⁻¹ ways to place cuts between characters and keep the splits where every piece is a palindrome. Time O(n · 2ⁿ), Space O(n).

:::details 1. Backtracking with a two-pointer palindrome check
`dfs(start)`: if `start == n`, the current pieces form a full partition. Otherwise, for each `end ≥ start`, check `s[start..end]` with two pointers moving inward, and only recurse when it's a palindrome, which prunes most branches early.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<String>> partition(String s) {
        List<List<String>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        dfs(s, 0, new ArrayList<>(), res);
        return res;
    }

    private void dfs(String s, int start, List<String> cur, List<List<String>> res) {
        if (start == s.length()) {
            res.add(new ArrayList<>(cur));
            return;
        }
        for (int end = start; end < s.length(); end++) {
            if (!isPal(s, start, end)) continue;
            cur.add(s.substring(start, end + 1));
            dfs(s, end + 1, cur, res);
            cur.remove(cur.size() - 1);
        }
    }

    private boolean isPal(String s, int i, int j) {
        while (i < j) if (s.charAt(i++) != s.charAt(j--)) return false;
        return true;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def partition(self, s: str) -> List[List[str]]:
        res, cur = [], []

        def is_pal(i, j):
            while i < j:
                if s[i] != s[j]:
                    return False
                i, j = i + 1, j - 1
            return True

        def dfs(start):
            if start == len(s):
                res.append(cur.copy())
                return
            for end in range(start, len(s)):
                if is_pal(start, end):
                    cur.append(s[start:end + 1])
                    dfs(end + 1)
                    cur.pop()

        dfs(0)
        return res
```

**Time:** O(n · 2ⁿ): up to 2ⁿ⁻¹ partitions, O(n) work per piece check/copy
**Space:** O(n) recursion (output excluded)
:::

:::details 2. Backtracking + DP palindrome table
Precompute `pal[i][j]` for every substring: `s[i] == s[j]` and the inside `s[i+1..j−1]` is a palindrome (or has length < 2). Fill i from the end so the inner value is ready. The backtracking then checks palindromes in O(1).

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<String>> partition(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        boolean[][] pal = new boolean[n][n]; // pal[i][j]: s[i..j] is a palindrome
        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--)
            for (int j = i; j < n; j++)
                pal[i][j] = s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j) && (j - i < 2 || pal[i + 1][j - 1]);
        List<List<String>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        dfs(s, pal, 0, new ArrayList<>(), res);
        return res;
    }

    private void dfs(String s, boolean[][] pal, int start, List<String> cur, List<List<String>> res) {
        if (start == s.length()) {
            res.add(new ArrayList<>(cur));
            return;
        }
        for (int end = start; end < s.length(); end++) {
            if (!pal[start][end]) continue;
            cur.add(s.substring(start, end + 1));
            dfs(s, pal, end + 1, cur, res);
            cur.remove(cur.size() - 1);
        }
    }
}
```

**Python**

