:::

**Pitfall:** Returning `[""]` for empty input. The expected answer is `[]`.

**Key idea:** Place one queen per row, top to bottom. Track which columns, diagonals (`r − c`) and anti-diagonals (`r + c`) are taken so each candidate square is checked in O(1), and backtrack when a row has no safe square.

**Brute force:** Since every row and column holds exactly one queen, try all n! column permutations and keep those where no two queens share a diagonal (approach 3). Time O(n · n!), Space O(n).

:::details 1. Backtracking with column / diagonal sets
`dfs(r)` tries each column `c` in row `r`. The square is safe if `c`, `r − c` and `r + c` are all unused. Mark them, place the queen, recurse to row `r + 1`, then unmark. Reaching row `n` means a full board.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<String>> solveNQueens(int n) {
        List<List<String>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        char[][] board = new char[n][n];
        for (char[] row : board) Arrays.fill(row, '.');
        // r - c is constant on a diagonal (shift by n), r + c on an anti-diagonal
        dfs(0, n, board, new boolean[n], new boolean[2 * n], new boolean[2 * n], res);
        return res;
    }

    private void dfs(int r, int n, char[][] board, boolean[] cols, boolean[] diag, boolean[] anti, List<List<String>> res) {
        if (r == n) {
            List<String> rows = new ArrayList<>();
            for (char[] row : board) rows.add(new String(row));
            res.add(rows);
            return;
        }
        for (int c = 0; c < n; c++) {
            if (cols[c] || diag[r - c + n] || anti[r + c]) continue;
            cols[c] = diag[r - c + n] = anti[r + c] = true;
            board[r][c] = 'Q';
            dfs(r + 1, n, board, cols, diag, anti, res);
            board[r][c] = '.';
            cols[c] = diag[r - c + n] = anti[r + c] = false;
        }
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def solveNQueens(self, n: int) -> List[List[str]]:
        cols, diag, anti = set(), set(), set()  # r - c is constant on a diagonal, r + c on an anti-diagonal
        board = [["."] * n for _ in range(n)]
        res = []

        def dfs(r):
            if r == n:
                res.append(["".join(row) for row in board])
                return
            for c in range(n):
                if c in cols or r - c in diag or r + c in anti:
                    continue
                cols.add(c); diag.add(r - c); anti.add(r + c)
                board[r][c] = "Q"
                dfs(r + 1)
                board[r][c] = "."
                cols.remove(c); diag.remove(r - c); anti.remove(r + c)

        dfs(0)
        return res
```

**Time:** O(n!) roughly; each row has fewer safe columns than the previous
**Space:** O(n²) for the board, O(n) for sets and recursion
:::

:::details 2. Backtracking with bitmasks
Same search, but the attacked columns are integer bitmasks. Moving down one row shifts the diagonal mask left and the anti-diagonal mask right. `free & -free` picks the lowest free column. Much faster constant factor.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private final List<List<String>> res = new ArrayList<>();
    private int n, full;
    private int[] queens; // queens[r] = column of the queen in row r

    public List<List<String>> solveNQueens(int n) {
        this.n = n;
        full = (1 << n) - 1;
        queens = new int[n];
        dfs(0, 0, 0, 0);
        return res;
    }

    // bit c set in diag/anti = column c is attacked diagonally in row r
    private void dfs(int r, int cols, int diag, int anti) {
        if (r == n) {
            List<String> rows = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int c : queens) {
                char[] row = new char[n];
                Arrays.fill(row, '.');
                row[c] = 'Q';
                rows.add(new String(row));
            }
            res.add(rows);
            return;
        }
        int free = full & ~(cols | diag | anti);
        while (free != 0) {
            int bit = free & -free; // lowest free column
            free -= bit;
            queens[r] = Integer.numberOfTrailingZeros(bit);
            dfs(r + 1, cols | bit, ((diag | bit) << 1) & full, (anti | bit) >> 1);
        }
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def solveNQueens(self, n: int) -> List[List[str]]:
        res, queens = [], []  # queens[r] = column of the queen in row r
        full = (1 << n) - 1

        def dfs(cols, diag, anti):
            # bit c set in diag/anti = column c is attacked diagonally in the current row
            if len(queens) == n:
                res.append(["." * c + "Q" + "." * (n - c - 1) for c in queens])
                return
            free = full & ~(cols | diag | anti)
            while free:
                bit = free & -free  # lowest free column
                free -= bit
                queens.append(bit.bit_length() - 1)
                dfs(cols | bit, ((diag | bit) << 1) & full, (anti | bit) >> 1)
                queens.pop()

        dfs(0, 0, 0)
        return res
```

**Time:** O(n!) roughly, with very cheap steps
**Space:** O(n) recursion
:::

:::details 3. Brute force: every column permutation
Generate all permutations of columns (queen in row r goes to column perm[r]), which already rules out shared rows and columns, then reject any with a repeated `r − c` or `r + c`.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private final List<List<String>> res = new ArrayList<>();

    public List<List<String>> solveNQueens(int n) {
        int[] perm = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) perm[i] = i;
        permute(perm, 0); // one queen per row and per column: try every column permutation
        return res;
    }

    private void permute(int[] p, int i) {
        if (i == p.length) {
            if (diagonalsOk(p)) res.add(render(p));
            return;
        }
        for (int j = i; j < p.length; j++) {
            int t = p[i]; p[i] = p[j]; p[j] = t;
            permute(p, i + 1);
            t = p[i]; p[i] = p[j]; p[j] = t;
        }
    }

    private boolean diagonalsOk(int[] p) {
        Set<Integer> d = new HashSet<>(), a = new HashSet<>();
        for (int r = 0; r < p.length; r++)
            if (!d.add(r - p[r]) || !a.add(r + p[r])) return false;
        return true;
    }

    private List<String> render(int[] p) {
        List<String> rows = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int c : p) {
            char[] row = new char[p.length];
            Arrays.fill(row, '.');
            row[c] = 'Q';
            rows.add(new String(row));
        }
        return rows;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
