:::

**Pitfall:** Forgetting to mark cells before recursing or enqueuing. Neighbours then revisit each other forever (DFS) or are counted several times (BFS).

**Key idea:** Traverse the graph once while keeping a map from each original node to its clone. The map lets you reuse clones on cycles and shared neighbours instead of copying forever.

**Brute force:** First collect all nodes (any traversal) and create a clone for each; in a second pass, for every original edge look up the clones by scanning the list of (original, clone) pairs. Time O(V·(V + E)) with linear lookups, Space O(V).

:::details 1. DFS with a hash map
`clone(n)`: if `n` was already copied, return the copy. Otherwise create the copy, store it in the map **before** recursing (otherwise a cycle would recurse forever), then clone each neighbour and attach it.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private final Map<Node, Node> copies = new HashMap<>(); // original node -> its clone

    public Node cloneGraph(Node node) {
        if (node == null) return null;
        if (copies.containsKey(node)) return copies.get(node);
        Node c = new Node(node.val);
        copies.put(node, c); // register before recursing, so cycles stop here
        for (Node nb : node.neighbors) c.neighbors.add(cloneGraph(nb));
        return c;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def cloneGraph(self, node: Optional['Node']) -> Optional['Node']:
        copies = {}  # original node -> its clone

        def clone(n):
            if n in copies:
                return copies[n]
            c = Node(n.val)
            copies[n] = c  # register before recursing, so cycles stop here
            c.neighbors = [clone(nb) for nb in n.neighbors]
            return c

        return clone(node) if node else None
```

**Time:** O(V + E)
**Space:** O(V) map + recursion depth
:::

:::details 2. BFS with a hash map
Clone the start node and put the original in a queue. For each popped node, make sure each neighbour has a clone (creating it and queueing the neighbour the first time), then append the neighbour's clone to the current clone's list.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public Node cloneGraph(Node node) {
        if (node == null) return null;
        Map<Node, Node> copies = new HashMap<>();
        copies.put(node, new Node(node.val));
        Deque<Node> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
        q.add(node);
        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            Node cur = q.poll();
            for (Node nb : cur.neighbors) {
                if (!copies.containsKey(nb)) {
                    copies.put(nb, new Node(nb.val));
                    q.add(nb);
                }
                copies.get(cur).neighbors.add(copies.get(nb));
            }
        }
        return copies.get(node);
    }
}
```

