**Pitfall:** Creating a new clone every time a node is reached. Cycles make that loop forever, and shared neighbours get duplicated.

**Key idea:** Multi-source BFS: put **all** gates in the queue at distance 0 and expand outward together. The first time a room is reached, it's from its nearest gate.

**Brute force:** For every empty room, run its own BFS until it hits a gate (approach 3). Time O((m·n)²), Space O(m·n).

:::details 1. Multi-source BFS (optimal)
Enqueue every gate. Pop a cell and look at its four neighbours; any neighbour still INF gets `dist + 1` and is enqueued. Because BFS goes in rings of increasing distance, each room is set exactly once to its smallest distance.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public void wallsAndGates(int[][] rooms) {
        final int INF = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        int rows = rooms.length, cols = rooms[0].length;
        Deque<int[]> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++)
                if (rooms[r][c] == 0) q.add(new int[]{r, c}); // all gates start together
        int[][] dirs = {{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}};
        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            int[] cell = q.poll();
            for (int[] d : dirs) {
                int nr = cell[0] + d[0], nc = cell[1] + d[1];
                if (nr >= 0 && nc >= 0 && nr < rows && nc < cols && rooms[nr][nc] == INF) {
                    rooms[nr][nc] = rooms[cell[0]][cell[1]] + 1; // first visit = shortest distance
                    q.add(new int[]{nr, nc});
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

