**Pitfall:** Running a separate BFS from each gate and overwriting rooms as you go: a later gate can replace a smaller distance with a larger one unless you compare first.

**Key idea:** Multi-source BFS from all rotten oranges at once; each BFS level is one minute. Count fresh oranges up front so you can tell at the end whether any were unreachable.

**Brute force:** Simulate minute by minute, rescanning the whole grid each minute for fresh oranges next to rotten ones (approach 2). Time O((m·n)²), Space O(m·n).

:::details 1. Multi-source BFS by levels (optimal)
Queue every rotten orange and count the fresh ones. While fresh oranges remain and the queue isn't empty, process one level (one minute): rot each fresh neighbour, decrement the count and enqueue it. At the end, any fresh oranges left mean −1.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int orangesRotting(int[][] grid) {
        int rows = grid.length, cols = grid[0].length, fresh = 0;
        Deque<int[]> q = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
                if (grid[r][c] == 2) q.add(new int[]{r, c});
                else if (grid[r][c] == 1) fresh++;
            }
        int[][] dirs = {{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}};
        int minutes = 0;
        while (!q.isEmpty() && fresh > 0) {
            minutes++;
            for (int i = q.size(); i > 0; i--) { // everything rotten at the start of this minute
                int[] cell = q.poll();
                for (int[] d : dirs) {
                    int nr = cell[0] + d[0], nc = cell[1] + d[1];
                    if (nr >= 0 && nc >= 0 && nr < rows && nc < cols && grid[nr][nc] == 1) {
                        grid[nr][nc] = 2;
                        fresh--;
                        q.add(new int[]{nr, nc});
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return fresh > 0 ? -1 : minutes;
    }
}
```

