:::

**Pitfall:** Counting the last BFS level as a minute even though it rotted nothing. Stop the loop once `fresh == 0` (or subtract one at the end).

**Key idea:** Reverse the flow. Start from each ocean's border cells and walk **uphill** (to neighbours with height ≥ current). That marks every cell that can drain into that ocean; the answer is the intersection of the two marked sets.

**Brute force:** From every cell, run a downhill DFS/BFS and check whether it touches both a Pacific edge and an Atlantic edge (approach 3). Time O((m·n)²), Space O(m·n).

:::details 1. DFS uphill from both oceans
Run a DFS from every top-row and left-column cell into a `pac` set, and from every bottom-row and right-column cell into an `atl` set, only stepping to neighbours that are at least as high. Each set is filled once, so each cell is visited at most twice overall.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private int[][] h;
    private int rows, cols;

    public List<List<Integer>> pacificAtlantic(int[][] heights) {
        h = heights;
        rows = h.length;
        cols = h[0].length;
        boolean[][] pac = new boolean[rows][cols], atl = new boolean[rows][cols];
        for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
            climb(0, c, pac);
            climb(rows - 1, c, atl);
        }
        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
            climb(r, 0, pac);
            climb(r, cols - 1, atl);
        }
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++)
                if (pac[r][c] && atl[r][c]) res.add(List.of(r, c));
        return res;
    }

    // walk "uphill" from the ocean: water could flow back down this way
    private void climb(int r, int c, boolean[][] seen) {
        if (seen[r][c]) return;
        seen[r][c] = true;
        int[][] dirs = {{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}};
        for (int[] d : dirs) {
            int nr = r + d[0], nc = c + d[1];
            if (nr >= 0 && nc >= 0 && nr < rows && nc < cols && !seen[nr][nc] && h[nr][nc] >= h[r][c])
                climb(nr, nc, seen);
        }
    }
}
```

