:::details 3. Brute force: downhill search from every cell
For each cell, explore everything reachable by flowing to equal-or-lower neighbours and record whether any reached cell is on a Pacific edge and any on an Atlantic edge.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> pacificAtlantic(int[][] heights) {
        int rows = heights.length, cols = heights[0].length;
        int[][] dirs = {{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}};
        List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int sr = 0; sr < rows; sr++) {
            for (int sc = 0; sc < cols; sc++) {
                // flow downhill from this cell and see which oceans it touches
                boolean pac = false, atl = false;
                boolean[][] seen = new boolean[rows][cols];
                Deque<int[]> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
                stack.push(new int[]{sr, sc});
                seen[sr][sc] = true;
                while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
                    int[] cell = stack.pop();
                    int r = cell[0], c = cell[1];
                    if (r == 0 || c == 0) pac = true;
                    if (r == rows - 1 || c == cols - 1) atl = true;
                    for (int[] d : dirs) {
                        int nr = r + d[0], nc = c + d[1];
                        if (nr >= 0 && nc >= 0 && nr < rows && nc < cols && !seen[nr][nc]
                                && heights[nr][nc] <= heights[r][c]) {
                            seen[nr][nc] = true;
                            stack.push(new int[]{nr, nc});
                        }
                    }
                }
                if (pac && atl) res.add(List.of(sr, sc));
            }
        }
        return res;
    }
}
```

