:::

:::details 2. DFS post-order with cycle detection
Point each course at its prerequisites. DFS with three states (unvisited / on path / done). When a course finishes, append it: all its prerequisites were appended earlier, so the list is already a valid order. Meeting an "on path" node means a cycle, so return `[]`.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private List<List<Integer>> graph;
    private int[] state; // 0 = unvisited, 1 = on path, 2 = done
    private List<Integer> order;

    public int[] findOrder(int numCourses, int[][] prerequisites) {
        graph = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < numCourses; i++) graph.add(new ArrayList<>());
        for (int[] p : prerequisites) graph.get(p[0]).add(p[1]);
        state = new int[numCourses];
        order = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int c = 0; c < numCourses; c++) if (!dfs(c)) return new int[0];
        return order.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).toArray();
    }

    private boolean dfs(int c) {
        if (state[c] == 1) return false; // cycle
        if (state[c] == 2) return true;
        state[c] = 1;
        for (int p : graph.get(c)) if (!dfs(p)) return false;
        state[c] = 2;
        order.add(c); // post-order: all prerequisites are already in order
        return true;
    }
}
```

