:::

**Pitfall:** Pointing DFS edges the other way (`b → a`) and appending in post-order without reversing. The order then comes out backwards.

**Key idea:** A graph on n nodes is a tree exactly when it has n − 1 edges **and** is connected (equivalently: n − 1 edges and no cycle).

**Brute force:** For every edge, remove it and check with a BFS whether its endpoints are still connected; if any edge is "redundant", there's a cycle. Then check connectivity once. Time O(E·(V + E)), Space O(V + E).

:::details 1. Union-Find
First reject anything without exactly n − 1 edges. Then union the endpoints of each edge; if two endpoints already share a root, that edge closes a cycle. With n − 1 edges and no cycle, the graph must be connected.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private int[] parent;

    public boolean validTree(int n, int[][] edges) {
        if (edges.length != n - 1) return false; // a tree on n nodes has exactly n - 1 edges
        parent = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;
        for (int[] e : edges) {
            int ra = find(e[0]), rb = find(e[1]);
            if (ra == rb) return false; // this edge closes a cycle
            parent[ra] = rb;
        }
        return true;
    }

    private int find(int x) {
        while (parent[x] != x) {
            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]];
            x = parent[x];
        }
        return x;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def validTree(self, n: int, edges: List[List[int]]) -> bool:
        if len(edges) != n - 1:
            return False  # a tree on n nodes has exactly n - 1 edges
        parent = list(range(n))

        def find(x):
            while parent[x] != x:
                parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]
                x = parent[x]
            return x

        for a, b in edges:
            ra, rb = find(a), find(b)
            if ra == rb:
                return False  # this edge closes a cycle
            parent[ra] = rb
        return True
```

**Time:** O(V + E · α(V))
**Space:** O(V)
:::

:::details 2. DFS with parent tracking
Build an adjacency list and DFS from node 0, skipping the edge back to the parent. Reaching an already visited node means a cycle. At the end, every node must have been visited (connected).

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private List<List<Integer>> graph;
    private boolean[] seen;
    private int count = 0;

    public boolean validTree(int n, int[][] edges) {
        graph = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) graph.add(new ArrayList<>());
        for (int[] e : edges) {
            graph.get(e[0]).add(e[1]);
            graph.get(e[1]).add(e[0]);
        }
        seen = new boolean[n];
        return dfs(0, -1) && count == n; // no cycle and connected
    }

    private boolean dfs(int node, int parent) {
        if (seen[node]) return false; // reached twice: cycle
        seen[node] = true;
        count++;
        for (int nb : graph.get(node))
            if (nb != parent && !dfs(nb, node)) return false;
        return true;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def validTree(self, n: int, edges: List[List[int]]) -> bool:
        graph = [[] for _ in range(n)]
        for a, b in edges:
            graph[a].append(b)
            graph[b].append(a)
        seen = set()

        def dfs(node, parent):
            if node in seen:
                return False  # reached twice: cycle
            seen.add(node)
            return all(dfs(nb, node) for nb in graph[node] if nb != parent)

        return dfs(0, -1) and len(seen) == n  # no cycle and connected
```

**Time:** O(V + E)
**Space:** O(V + E)
:::

:::details 3. Edge count + BFS connectivity
If the edge count is n − 1, a single BFS from node 0 that reaches all n nodes proves it's a tree (a connected graph with n − 1 edges can't have a cycle).

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean validTree(int n, int[][] edges) {
        if (edges.length != n - 1) return false;
        List<List<Integer>> graph = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) graph.add(new ArrayList<>());
        for (int[] e : edges) {
            graph.get(e[0]).add(e[1]);
            graph.get(e[1]).add(e[0]);
        }
        boolean[] seen = new boolean[n];
        seen[0] = true;
        Deque<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>(List.of(0));
        int count = 1;
        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            for (int nb : graph.get(q.poll())) {
                if (!seen[nb]) {
                    seen[nb] = true;
                    count++;
                    q.add(nb);
                }
            }
        }
        return count == n; // n - 1 edges + connected => tree
    }
}
```

