:::

**Pitfall:** In the undirected DFS, treating the edge back to the node you just came from as a cycle. Pass the parent along and skip it.

**Key idea:** Start with n separate components. Every edge that joins two different components merges them (Union-Find), or count how many times you have to start a fresh DFS/BFS.

**Brute force:** For each pair of nodes, check reachability with a BFS and group nodes that reach each other. Time O(V² · (V + E)), Space O(V + E).

:::details 1. Union-Find (rank + path compression)
Begin with `components = n`. For each edge, find both roots; if they differ, union them and decrement the count. Edges inside an existing component change nothing.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private int[] parent, rank;

    public int countComponents(int n, int[][] edges) {
        parent = new int[n];
        rank = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;
        int components = n;
        for (int[] e : edges) {
            int ra = find(e[0]), rb = find(e[1]);
            if (ra == rb) continue;
            if (rank[ra] < rank[rb]) { int t = ra; ra = rb; rb = t; }
            parent[rb] = ra;
            if (rank[ra] == rank[rb]) rank[ra]++;
            components--; // two pieces merged into one
        }
        return components;
    }

    private int find(int x) {
        while (parent[x] != x) {
            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]];
            x = parent[x];
        }
        return x;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def countComponents(self, n: int, edges: List[List[int]]) -> int:
        parent, rank = list(range(n)), [0] * n

        def find(x):
            while parent[x] != x:
                parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]
                x = parent[x]
            return x

        components = n
        for a, b in edges:
            ra, rb = find(a), find(b)
            if ra == rb:
                continue
            if rank[ra] < rank[rb]:
                ra, rb = rb, ra
            parent[rb] = ra
            if rank[ra] == rank[rb]:
                rank[ra] += 1
            components -= 1  # two pieces merged into one
        return components
```

**Time:** O(V + E · α(V))
**Space:** O(V)
:::

:::details 2. DFS from every unvisited node
Build an adjacency list. Scan nodes 0 … n − 1; whenever one is unvisited, that's a new component, so count it and DFS to mark everything it reaches.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private List<List<Integer>> graph;
    private boolean[] seen;

    public int countComponents(int n, int[][] edges) {
        graph = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) graph.add(new ArrayList<>());
        for (int[] e : edges) {
            graph.get(e[0]).add(e[1]);
            graph.get(e[1]).add(e[0]);
        }
        seen = new boolean[n];
        int count = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (!seen[i]) {
                count++; // new component
                dfs(i);
            }
        }
        return count;
    }

    private void dfs(int node) {
        seen[node] = true;
        for (int nb : graph.get(node)) if (!seen[nb]) dfs(nb);
    }
}
```

